--- body ---
## Context

**Distinct from, and complementary to, ticket 0852 (PR #786, open at filing
time).** 0852 fixes a *worktree-creation* failure: `git worktree add` at
gaze Phase 1 can be silently denied with no exit-status check, so the run
falls back into the invoking tree before any reviewer agent is even
spawned. This ticket's defect fires even when Phase 1 SUCCEEDS — one of the
observed gaze runs below explicitly logged "Review worktree
`.../review-344` already existed... reused as-is", i.e. the worktree WAS
there. The failure is one layer further in: the non-isolated reviewer/gate
subagents that Phase 1 hands that worktree off to have no platform-level
way to actually move into it. 0852's Action 1 (hard-fail on creation
failure) would not catch this — creation had already succeeded.

Ticket 0233 (closed) mandates that `/gaze`'s phases 2-4 reviewer agents and
the phase-6 gate agent must be **non-isolated** (pinned-cwd, read-only,
`isolation:"worktree"` explicitly forbidden) — the original 0216 bug was
that isolating them cut a fresh tree from `main`, losing the actual PR
content. The documented mitigation is: gaze itself creates a `review-NNN`
worktree once, and prompts each reviewer subagent to work pinned to that
path.

That mitigation rests on an assumption that turns out to be false: a
non-isolated Agent-tool subagent's cwd is not merely "defaulted" to the
parent's worktree, it is **hard-pinned** there at the platform level, with
no legitimate escape hatch to a different worktree — including the one
`/gaze` itself creates for exactly this purpose.

**Reproduced directly** (search-works-for-zotero session, raid on tickets
638/640, 2026-09-04). A plain `general-purpose` Agent-tool subagent, spawned
with no `isolation` parameter, launched already parked in the PARENT
session's own worktree (`.claude/worktrees/raid-t638-t640`) rather than in
any repo-appropriate default. It then called `EnterWorktree(name:
"diag-probe-isolation-test")` to test whether it could move to a fresh
directory, and got an explicit, load-bearing refusal:

    EnterWorktree cannot create a worktree from a subagent with a cwd
    override (isolation: "worktree" or explicit cwd) — it would mutate the
    parent session's process-wide working directory. To switch this agent
    into an existing worktree managed by Claude Code (under
    .claude/worktrees/ of this repository), call EnterWorktree with `path`.

So a non-isolated subagent cannot create a new worktree, and per the error
text can only reach an *existing, already-registered* Claude-Code-managed
worktree via `EnterWorktree(path: ...)` — not via a bare `cd`, not via
`git -C`, and the tool never mentions this `path` affordance being reachable
without first knowing the exact existing worktree's registered path.

**Consequence, observed live in the same session, multiple independent
times**, across two separate `/gaze` invocations on two different PRs:

- Three different reviewer subagents (adherence/Agent A, `/review`/Agent B,
  the phase-6 gate agent) each independently reported: "the directive's
  stated path `.../worktrees/review-NNN` does not exist" or "I was unable to
  leave this session's own worktree (`raid-t638-t640`) despite
  `EnterWorktree` reporting success" — then proceeded to run their checks
  directly in the PARENT session's (the raid orchestrator's) own worktree,
  reporting this as if it were an acceptable fallback rather than a
  containment failure.
- One reviewer subagent, needing to inspect a different commit's content
  ("read-only" by design), reflexively ran `git checkout <ref> -- .` in
  that pinned cwd — which is the PARENT's real, shared worktree, not a
  disposable review copy. This **silently discarded an uncommitted,
  unstaged edit** sitting in that worktree at the time (recovered without
  loss only because the orchestrator happened to still hold the exact
  content in its own conversation context).
- A `/gaze` setup-phase log entry on a later invocation for the same PR
  independently confirmed the shape: "Review worktree
  `$primary_root/.claude/worktrees/review-344` already existed from an
  earlier, apparently orphaned partial gaze/review pass... reused as-is" —
  i.e. gaze's own worktree bookkeeping does not reliably persist or get
  found by the reviewer subagents it spawns, even within the same overall
  run.

**Net effect:** every `/gaze` review currently believed to run against an
isolated `review-NNN` copy is, in practice, at meaningful risk of running
directly inside whichever worktree the calling session happens to occupy —
silently, with no error surfaced to the caller, and with at least one
observed case of that "read-only" review destructively mutating the
caller's real working tree.

## Relevant files

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — phases 2-4/6 agent-spawn prompts; the
  "pinned cwd, non-isolation" contract from ticket 0233
- `tickets/closed/0233-ratchet-test-gaze-reviewer-agents-must-s.erg` — the
  ratchet that pins non-isolation, whose premise this ticket challenges
- `tickets/closed/0221-fang-audit-runtime-per-agent-worktree-is.erg` — prior
  art on the same platform-level default (`Workflow agent()` calls landing
  in the session checkout unless isolation is explicitly requested)
- `tests/test_verify_fork_contracts.py` — the 0193/0233 ratchet suite that
  currently only checks gaze's *text* states the non-isolation contract,
  not that reviewer agents can actually reach the worktree the contract
  assumes exists

## Actions

1. Reproduce cleanly inside this repo (a controlled `Workflow`/`Agent`
   fan-out spawning a non-isolated child, told to reach a pre-created
   sibling worktree by path) to confirm the failure mode outside the
   search-works-for-zotero session that first surfaced it.
2. Decide the fix shape — options, not yet arbitrated:
   - a. Give gaze's reviewer prompts the `EnterWorktree(path: ...)` recipe
     explicitly (register the review worktree, then instruct every
     reviewer to call `EnterWorktree` with that exact path as its first
     action) rather than a bare "cd into this directory" instruction.
   - b. If (a) still fails for nested/forked callers (gaze itself runs as a
     forked skill, so its reviewer spawns are two levels deep from the
     interactive session) — reconsider whether reviewer agents can be
     pinned-cwd at all, or whether they need `isolation:"worktree"` after
     all, paired with an explicit `git fetch`+`checkout <PR-head-sha>`
     step in their prompt to recover 0216's original guarantee without
     the 0233 drift risk.
   - c. At minimum, make the failure LOUD: a reviewer subagent that cannot
     reach its assigned worktree should refuse and report NOT-RUN rather
     than silently falling back to the parent's cwd.
3. Whatever the fix, forbid `git checkout <ref> -- .` (or any working-tree
   overwrite) from a "read-only" reviewer's toolset/prompt entirely — use
   `git show <ref>:<path>` or `git diff` to inspect without writing, per
   `rules/git.md`'s existing guidance on this exact trap.

## Test

Red-first: a fixture that spawns a non-isolated Agent-tool child, gives it
a `review-*` worktree path in its prompt exactly as gaze does today, and
asserts the child actually operates there (e.g. writes a marker file whose
location is checked from the parent) — must fail against gaze's current
prompt-only pinning, pass once (a) or (b) above lands.

## Verification

- [ ] The reproduction in Actions item 1 is captured as a committed fixture
      or script, not just narrated
- [ ] Whichever fix is chosen actually lands a non-isolated reviewer agent
      inside the intended separate worktree, verified by the red-first test
- [ ] No reviewer/gate-agent prompt anywhere in `skills/gaze/` can reach a
      command that overwrites tracked files in place (`checkout -- .`,
      `restore .`, `reset --hard`) in a shared worktree
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Ticket 0216's original guarantee stays intact: a reviewer must see the
  actual PR content, not a fresh-from-main checkout.
- A reviewer/gate agent's read-only status is enforced by what it CAN do,
  not only by what its prompt tells it to do — the destructive-checkout
  incident happened despite an explicit "read-only" framing.

## Exit criteria

`/gaze` reviewer and gate agents reliably operate against the PR's actual
content in a genuinely isolated worktree — or, if that turns out to be
architecturally unreachable for non-isolated subagents on this platform,
the design is changed to one that is, rather than continuing to rely on a
pinned-cwd contract now shown to fail silently and destructively.
